  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Rhodotus palmatus, commonly known as the Wrinkled Peach, is a small woodland mushroom characterized by its distinctive pink to peachy-colored caps with a unique wrinkled or veined surface pattern. This fungus belongs to the Physalacriaceae family and is found across New England, typically fruiting in late spring through fall.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Wrinkled Cap Surface",
      "description": "The cap features a striking pattern of radial ridges and wrinkles that resemble palm prints or brain-like folds, giving the mushroom its distinctive appearance",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pink to Peach Coloration",
      "description": "Fresh specimens display a characteristic pale pink to peachy or salmon-colored cap that may fade with age or drying",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Caps typically measure 1-3 cm in diameter with slender, fragile stems, making this a delicate and relatively inconspicuous mushroom",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Rhodotus palmatus grows on decaying hardwood logs and woody debris in moist forest environments throughout New England, preferring shaded woodland floors.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The wrinkled pattern on its cap is so distinctive that mycologists use it as the primary identifying feature, and the mushroom's scientific name 'palmatus' literally refers to this palm-like or hand-like appearance of the ridged surface.",
